Beats me why the condition is written like that:

if (mdat->msize < MZ_HUMAN && typ != FOOD_RATION
     && typ != LEASH
     && typ != FIGURINE
     && (otmp->owt > 3 ||
     objects[typ].oc_big /*oc_bimanual/oc_bulky*/ ||
     is_spear(otmp) || is_pole(otmp) ||
     typ == MORNING_STAR))
     delobj(otmp);

So the item is not created if:

it's weight is over 3 or
it's bimanual or
it's bulky or
it's a spear or
it's a polearm or
it's a morningstar unless
it's a food ration or
it's a leash or
it's a figurine

Food ration weights 5 so it would be excluded, figurines curiously 
enough weight 50 and leash 12. No food item nor any non-weapon 
non-container tool is bimaunual or bulky. So the exclusion is stricktly 
for the weight.

So one could change it to:

Large object is defined here as weighting more than 3, is bimanual or 
bulky, is a spear, a polearm or a morning star. Food rations, leashes 
and figurines are not considered large objects although their weight is 
more than 3.

I btw. like to have the definition say 'is bimanual' or 'is bulky' 
instead of just 'is big', as big is used to denote either bimanual or 
bulky objects.

